.TH CVEC_DATA_PTR 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_data_ptr \- get a pointer to a vector's contiguous backing store
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

void *p = cvec_data_ptr(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A wrapper for cvector_data_ptr() that gives a pointer to the start of the
vector's contiguous backing store, so you can hand the elements to any C
interface that expects a plain array. The pointer leads to the vector's
own storage, not a copy, and writing through it changes the content of
the vector.

The result is a void *; assign it to a pointer of the vector's element
type at the call site.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v    Vector to get the data pointer from
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the first element of the backing store.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer covers the whole capacity of the vector, but only the first
cvec_size(3) elements hold values.

Don't free this pointer; the vector owns it and frees it when it is
destroyed.

The macro asserts when v is NULL.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Any operation that can grow or shrink the backing store reallocates it
and so invalidates the pointer. Those are cvec_push(3),
cvec_append_array(3), cvec_append_cvec(3), cvec_reserve(3),
cvec_reset(3), cvec_pop(3) and the destruction; cvec_pop(3) shrinks the
store when the vector drops below a quarter full. Fetch the pointer again
after any of them instead of keeping the old value.

Passing this pointer straight back into cvec_append_array(3) or
cvector_append_array(3) is safe, because those functions read their
source through any reallocation that they start.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 10);
cvec_push(vec, 20);

int *arr = cvec_data_ptr(vec);
for (size_t i = 0; i < cvec_size(vec); i++) {
    printf("%d\en", arr[i]);
}
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_data_ptr (3),
.BR cvec_at_ptr (3),
.BR cvec_size (3)
